$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM (3S,4S,5R,6S)-methyl-2-bromo-5-(tert-butyldimethylsilyloxy)-7-(tertbutyldiphenylsilyloxy)-
3-hydroxy-2,4,6-trimethylheptanoate (S12a,S12b) . To a cold (-78°C)
solution of aldehyde S11 (1 equiv.) in dry CH2Cl2 (0.1 M) was added
bromoenoxysilane 24 (1.3 equiv.). The mixture was stirred for 1 min at
-78°C then BF3 .OEt (1.5 equiv.) was added slowly. The resulting solution
was stirred for 1 hour until the aldehyde was completely consumed, as
determined by TLC. A saturated aqueous solution of NH4Cl was poured into
the reaction mixture. After the aqueous layer was extracted with ether,
the organic layer was successively washed with a saturated aqueous
solution of NaHCO3 and brine. The organic layer was dried (MgSO4),
filtered, and concentrated. The residue was purified by flash
chromatography on silica gel using 10 % EtOAc-hexane to give the desired
compound S12a,S12b (yield = 84%). The ratio 2,3- diastereoisomers was
found to be >20 : 1 on the basis of NMR data.
(3S,4S,5R,6S)-methyl-2-bromo-5-(tert-butyldimethylsilyloxy)-7-(tertbutyldiphenylsilyloxy)-
3-hydroxy-2,4,6-trimethylheptanoate (S12a,S12b) (yield = 84%). Colorless
oil. Rf= 0.25 (hexanes:EtOAc, 95:5) ; IR (neat) νmax = 3565, 3071, 2954,
1739, 1467, 1256; 1H NMR (400 MHz, CDCl3) δ 7.67-7.65 (m, 4H), 7.45-7.36
(m, 6H), 4.26 (d, J= 3.7 Hz, 1H), 3.90 (dd, J= 2.5, 5.3 Hz, 1H), 3.73 (s,
3H), 3.52-3.42 (m, 2H), 2.63 (d, J= 4.1 Hz, 1H), 1.93-1.90 (m, 4H)
1.72-1.72 (m, 1H), 1.26-1.24 (m, 1H), 1.07 (s, 3H), 0.97 (d, J= 7.0 Hz,
3H), 0.88 (s, 3H), 0.80 (d, J= 6.8 Hz, 3H), 0.07 (s, 3H), 0.01 (s, 3H);
13C NMR (100 MHz, CDCl3) 171.4, 135.8, 134.1, 129.9, 129.8, 127.93,
127.87, 75.1, 74.2, 68.6, 67.2, 53.4, 40.1, 38.1, 27.1, 26.3, 24.2, 19.4,
18.5, 11.8, 10.5; MS (ESI) m/z 687.3 (M+Na+, 100), 665.3 (MH+, 60), 279.1
(38); HRMS calcd for C33H54BrO5Si2 (MH+): 665.2693, found: 665.2705 (1.2
ppm).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM stereoselective, Mukaiyama Aldolization
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Stereopentads Derived from a Sequence of Mukaiyama Aldolization and Free
Radical Reduction on α-Methyl-β-alkoxy Aldehydes: A General Strategy for
Efficient Polypropionate Synthesis
